          <h2 style="border: none !important; margin-top: 0 !important; padding-bottom: 0 !important;">Frequently Asked Questions About Souk Bargaining</h2>
          
          <h3 class="roam-faq-question">Is bargaining expected in the Marrakech souks?</h3>
          <p class="roam-faq-answer">Yes. In Moroccan souks, bargaining is standard practice for artisan crafts, leatherwork, carpets, and spices. It is not considered impolite; rather, it is viewed as an interactive dialogue and an opening of human acquaintance. Fixed prices only apply in supermarkets, modern boutiques, pharmacy cooperatives with stamped pricing, and upscale restaurants.</p>

          <h3 class="roam-faq-question">Does drinking the merchant's mint tea obligate me to buy something?</h3>
          <p class="roam-faq-answer">No. In Moroccan and Amazigh hospitality, accepting a glass of mint tea signifies mutual respect and welcome, never a legal or moral contract to make a purchase. If you sit, drink tea, enjoy the conversation, and decide not to buy, you simply say 'Shukran bzaf' (Thank you very much) with your hand on your heart and depart gracefully.</p>

          <h3 class="roam-faq-question">Should you always offer half of the merchant's first price?</h3>
